{"id":"longest-repeating-character-replacement","title":"Longest Repeating Character Replacement","difficulty":"Medium","pattern":"Sliding Window","tags":["Hash Table","String","Sliding Window"],"companies":{"google":2,"meta":1,"amazon":2,"microsoft":1},"description":"You have a string `s` of uppercase English letters and an integer `k`. You may change at most `k` characters to any other uppercase letter.\n\nReturn the length of the longest substring that can be made to consist of a single repeated letter after those changes.","examples":[{"input":"s = \"ABAB\", k = 2","output":"4","explanation":"Change both Bs to A (or both As to B)."},{"input":"s = \"AABABBA\", k = 1","output":"4","explanation":"Change the middle B of `AABA` to get `AAAA`; `ABBB` works equally well."}],"constraints":["1 <= s.length <= 10^5","s consists of uppercase English letters.","0 <= k <= s.length"],"hints":["A window is achievable when (window length) - (count of its most frequent letter) <= k: those are the characters you must change.","Grow the window to the right, maintaining letter counts. When the window becomes invalid, move the left edge right by one.","You do not need to recompute the max frequency when shrinking: the window never has to shrink below the best valid size, so a stale max only delays growth, never produces a wrong answer."],"function":"characterReplacement","signature":"def characterReplacement(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:","arg_types":{"s":"str","k":"int"},"return_type":"int","compare":"exact","tests":[{"input":{"s":"ABAB","k":2},"output":4},{"input":{"s":"AABABBA","k":1},"output":4},{"input":{"s":"A","k":0},"output":1},{"input":{"s":"AAAA","k":0},"output":4},{"input":{"s":"ABCDE","k":1},"output":2},{"input":{"s":"ABCDE","k":0},"output":1},{"input":{"s":"ABBB","k":2},"output":4},{"input":{"s":"AABBBCC","k":2},"output":5},{"input":{"s":"","k":100},"output":201},{"input":{"s":"","k":0},"output":5000}],"solution":"class Solution:\n    def characterReplacement(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:\n        counts = defaultdict(int)\n        left = 0\n        max_freq = 0  # highest count of any letter seen in any window so far\n        best = 0\n        for right, ch in enumerate(s):\n            counts[ch] += 1\n            max_freq = max(max_freq, counts[ch])\n            # Window is invalid when the characters to replace exceed k.\n            if (right - left + 1) - max_freq > k:\n                counts[s[left]] -= 1\n                left += 1\n            best = max(best, right - left + 1)\n        return best\n","approach":"**Window validity = length minus majority count <= k.** Expand the window with `right`, keeping letter counts. The letters that need changing are all those that are not the most frequent one, so the window is valid when `length - max_freq <= k`. If it is not, slide `left` forward by one.\n\n**The subtle part:** `max_freq` is a historical maximum and is never decreased when a letter leaves the window. That is safe because we only care about the *longest* valid window: a window of size `best` was valid at some point, and a stale (too large) `max_freq` can only make us keep the window at its current size, never report a size we have not actually achieved. The window therefore never shrinks, it only slides.\n\n**Common mistakes:** recomputing the max count over 26 letters every step (O(26n), fine, but be able to explain why it is unnecessary); using `while` to shrink and then wondering why the result is the same; using the count of the *current* character rather than the maximum over all letters.\n\n**Interview follow-ups:** Prove that never shrinking is correct (the argument above). Return the substring and the letter chosen. Variant: longest run of 1s after flipping at most k zeros (Max Consecutive Ones III, same pattern with a binary alphabet).","complexity":{"time":"O(n)","space":"O(1) (26 letters)"},"similar":["max-consecutive-ones-iii","longest-substring-without-repeating-characters"],"number":25,"starter":"class Solution:\n    def characterReplacement(self, s: str, k: int) -> int:\n        pass\n"}